  "teaching": {
    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M6 V17: Back emf in DC motors. Why does a spinning DC motor draw less current? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "The spinning coil is a loop of wire turning in a magnetic field. As it turns, the flux through it keeps changing, and from Faraday's law, a changing flux induces an emf. So the motor's coil is also a generator. Which way does this emf point? Lenz's law says it opposes the change that causes it. The rotation is driven by the supply's current, so the induced emf pushes against the supply. We call it the back emf. Back emf is the emf induced in a motor's spinning coil, opposing the supply voltage. It isn't an extra part. It comes from the motion itself. Why against, and not with? Use the same energy argument as Lenz's law, with the supply still connected. Suppose the induced emf helped the supply, adding, say, ten volts to the twelve. Then twenty two volts would drive eleven amps through the two ohm coil. The supply would put in twelve volts times eleven amps, one hundred and thirty two watts. But the coil's resistance alone would turn two hundred and forty two watts into heat, while the motor kept speeding up. More energy out than in. That's impossible, so the back emf must oppose the supply. Then the supply has to do work, pushing each coulomb of charge through the coil against the back emf. And that work is exactly the energy that becomes the motor's mechanical work. Let's put that into the circuit. Picture the coil as two things in series. Its resistance, R, and the back emf, pointing against the supply. Going round the loop, the supply voltage V is shared between them. Part of it balances the back emf, and the rest drives the current through the resistance, I R. So V equals the back emf plus I R. Rearrange, and the current is the supply voltage minus the back emf, all over R. Only the voltage that's left over drives the current.",
