ent circuit** (coded supply, resistor R and a rose back-emf cell pointing against the supply, with a voltage-share bar beside it: V split into ε_back and IR); **current-speed graph** (coded `lineGraph`: I from 6.0 A at ω = 0 falling linearly to 1.0 A at full speed, a moving dot); **power bars** (input splits into heat and converted, with a thin \"friction\" sliver off the converted bar); **jam block** (coded block against the flywheel); **rheostat stepper** (the rheostat plate's slider moving in steps, a coded bypass link closing); `AlignedAlgebra`; `Countdown` (3 s in scene 1; 10 s in scenes 11 and 13); `QuestionCard`; `Chip` with `cross`; `KeyNotes`; `Heading`.\n- **Board layout:** heading top-left; the motor bench or the motor model centre-left; the equivalent circuit, graph or power bars on the right; calculation strips (task, equation, grouped givens) in the right column when numbers are worked; everything above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Author notes (not learner copy):**\n  (a) Model: fixed field (permanent magnets), constant winding resistance, inductance and brush drop ignored, steady states only; ε_back ∝ ω. The spin-up animation is illustrative (no times on screen). Revision 2 (brief 190 optional): the 6.0 A is the resistance-only estimate just after switch-on, before appreciable rotation; the coil's inductance (v12) makes the current rise over a short time, which this model ignores. Narration says \"just after the switch closes, before the coil has turned much\" and \"just as it starts\", and the graph's ω = 0 point carries the tag \"resistance-only estimate\".\n  (b) Values: V = 12 V, R = 2.0 Ω; full speed ε_back = 10 V, I = 1.0 A; half speed ε_back = 5.0 V, I = 3.5 A (a hypothetical steady state at half speed, as in the site's Stop and Check; torque at 3.5 A is 3.5 times the full-speed torque); start and jam I = 6.0 A; resistor 4.0 Ω gives 2.0 A.\n  (c) Ledger at full speed: VI = 12 W; I²R = 2.0 W; ε_backI = 10 W; 10/12 = 83 per cent (83.3). At half speed (author check, not narrated): 42 W = 24.5 W + 17.5 W. Jammed: 72 W, all heat; 72/2.0 = 36.\n  (d) Back emf direction: the induced emf in the turning coil drives (or would drive) a current whose motor-effect torque opposes the rotation; in the circuit it acts against the supply current. The overlay shows it as a rose arrow along the coil pointing against the orange current arrow.\n  (e) Pronunciations: \"emf\" letter by letter; \"back emf\" as \"back E M F\"; \"ohms\"; \"I R\" as \"eye ar\"; \"I squared R\" as \"eye squared ar\"; \"V I\" as \"vee eye\".\n  (f) The what-if is counterfactual and greyed with a \"what if\" tag. Revision 2 (brief 190 blocker): it now carries values that respect the connected supply. With V = 12 V, R = 2.0 Ω and a hypothetical assisting 10 V emf, I = (12 + 10)/2.0 = 11 A; supply power VI = 132 W; Joule heating I²R = 242 W, already 110 W more than the supply provides before any kinetic energy is gained. So a helping emf with forward motor torque would violate energy conservation. With the real opposing emf, VI = I²R + ε_back I and the conversion term ε_back I is positive (electrical to mechanical).\n  (g) More load does not always mean more mechanical power (Revision 2, brief 190 should fix). At constant 12 V and 2.0 Ω the converted power is ε_back(12 − ε_back)/2.0: 10 W at ε_back = 10 V, 17.5 W at 5.0 V, but only 5.5 W at 1.0 V near stall, even though current and torque keep rising. Feedback 2 therefore says only that the larger input splits into converted power and heat, and that the heat has grown.\n- **Open decisions for root:**\n  - **1. v16 join.** The opening assumes v16 ends on its coded DC motor model turning steadily, with a chip \"Next: why does a spinning DC motor draw less current?\". If v16's final frame differs (for example, if it already shows an ammeter or a bench), root replaces scene 1's first sentence and the opening camera move; nothing else depends on it.\n  - **2. Motor plates.** Closed in Revision 2: `y12p